    "padaccheda": "तत्र, अद्वैत-सिद्धेः, द्वैत-मिथ्यात्व-सिद्धि-पूर्वकत्वात्, द्वैत-मिथ्यात्वम्, एव, प्रथमम्, उपपादनीयम्, विप्रतिपत्ति-विचारः, ब्रह्म-प्रमा-अतिरिक्त-अबाध्यत्वे, सति, सत्त्वेन, प्रतीति-अर्हम्, चित्-भिन्नम्, प्रतिपन्न-उपाधौ, त्रैकालिक-निषेध-प्रतियोगि, न, वा",
    "anvaya": "अद्वैतसिद्धेर्द्वैतमिथ्यात्वसिद्धिपूर्वकत्वात् द्वैतमिथ्यात्वमेव प्रथममुपपादनीयम् । तत्र मध्यस्थेन विप्रतिपत्तिः प्रदर्शनीया — ब्रह्मप्रमातिरिक्ताबाध्यत्वे सति सत्त्वेन प्रतीत्यर्हं चिद्भिन्नं प्रतिपन्नोपाधौ त्रैकालिकनिषेधप्रतियोगि न वा इति ।",
    "bhashyaExtract": "अद्वैतसिद्धिर्द्वैतमिथ्यात्वसिद्धिसापेक्षेति प्रथमं मिथ्यात्वमुपपाद्यते । विप्रतिपत्तेश्च न पक्षतासम्पादकत्वं किन्तु विचाराङ्गत्वम् । सिद्धिसाधन-बाधवारणाय 'ब्रह्मप्रमातिरिक्ताबाध्यत्वे सति' इत्यादीनि त्रीणि विशेषणानि पक्षे निवेशितानि ।",
    "englishTranslation": "Inquiry into the Contested Thesis (Vipratipatti-vicāraḥ): Because the establishment of Non-duality (Advaitasiddhi) presupposes proving the falsity of duality, the falsity of duality must be demonstrated first. Since proof consists in establishing one's own thesis and refuting the opponent's, both must proceed through one of the three modes of debate—Vāda (truth-seeking dialogue), Jalpa (disputation), or Vitaṇḍā (critique). Here, the arbiter (madhyastha) must first state the contested proposition (vipratipatti) so that the inquiry has a determinate focus—not as a prerequisite for pakṣatā (which requires only absence of certainty without desire to infer), nor merely to assign sides, but as an ancillary discipline defining the subject of dispute.\n\nAccordingly, the formal Vipratipatti on falsity is framed thus: 'Is everything distinct from Pure Consciousness—which, while being unsublated by any cognition other than Brahman-realization, is fit to be apprehended as real—the counter-positive of triple-time (past, present, future) negation within the very locus wherein it appears, or is it not? Or, is it the counter-positive of such negation characterized by absolute reality (pāramārthikatva), or not?' Here, 'unsublated by anything other than Brahman-knowledge' prevents proving what is already admitted (siddha-sādhana) in shell-silver; 'fit to be apprehended as real' excludes the utterly non-existent (tuccha, like a hare's horn); and 'distinct from Consciousness' prevents sublation (bādha) in Brahman. Or individual entities like ether or earth may be taken separately as subjects without incurring the fallacy of uncertain deviation (sandigdhānaikāntikatva).",
    "commentaryNotes": "Madhusūdana immediately answers Vyāsatīrtha's opening attack in Nyāyāmṛta against the formal framing of the Advaita debate, precisely tightening the pakṣa (subject) qualifications so that neither shell-silver (prātibhāsika), nor non-being (tuccha), nor Brahman falls within scope awkwardly.",
